<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Masinissa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 238–<a href="/years/148-bce">148 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> · Also known as Massinissa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Masinissa was the first king of united Numidia. Son of Gaia of the Massylii, he fought for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> but went over to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 206 BCE, defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syphax">Syphax</a> and led the Numidian cavalry at Zama in 202 BCE. He then united Numidia with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cirta">Cirta</a>, encouraged farming and towns, and steadily encroached on Carthaginian territory.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/masinissa.jpg" alt="Masinissa" width="320" height="388" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bust_of_Masinissa_-_Capitoline_Museum.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Masinissa, whose name appears on his tomb as MSNSN, was the son of Gaia, chieftain of the Numidian Massylii, and was brought up in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a>, his father's ally. Much of what is known of him comes from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livy">Livy</a> and, to a lesser extent, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a>'s Scipio's Dream. At the start of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-punic-war">Second Punic War</a> he fought for Carthage against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syphax">Syphax</a>, king of the western Masaesyli, who had allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, and won a decisive victory. He was betrothed to the daughter of the Carthaginian general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hasdrubal-gisco">Hasdrubal Gisco</a>, then commanded Numidian cavalry in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, where he was placed in charge of all Carthaginian horse and fought a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> campaign against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scipio-africanus">Scipio Africanus</a> in 208 and 207.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When Gaia died in 206 BCE, Masinissa quarrelled with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oezalces">Oezalces</a>, his father's brother, over the inheritance, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syphax">Syphax</a> took much of eastern Numidia. Concluding that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> was winning the war, Masinissa defected and promised to help Scipio invade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. Hasdrubal turned to Syphax, who married his daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sophonisba">Sophonisba</a>. Masinissa fought at the Battle of the Great Plains and Bagbrades in 203, and with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaius-laelius">Gaius Laelius</a> pursued Syphax to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cirta">Cirta</a>, where he was captured. Masinissa married Sophonisba, but Scipio demanded that she be shown in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-triumph">Roman triumph</a>, and Masinissa sent her poison, with which she killed herself.</p><p>At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-zama">Battle of Zama</a> he commanded the cavalry on Scipio's right wing, 6,000 Numidian and 3,000 Roman, and his return against the rear of the Carthaginian lines decided the battle. He received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syphax">Syphax</a>'s kingdom and ruled both the Massylii and the Masaesyli. With <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cirta">Cirta</a> as his capital he sought to build a unified state from semi-nomadic tribes, introduced Carthaginian farming methods and settled many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/numidians">Numidians</a> as peasants. Major towns included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gafsa">Capsa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dougga">Thugga</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulla-regia">Bulla Regia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hippo-regius">Hippo Regius</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The peace treaty of 201 forbade <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> to make war without Roman permission, which let Masinissa encroach on its territory. He defeated the Carthaginians at the Battle of Oroscopa in 151 BCE. Carthage's retaliation against Numidian raids, in defiance of the treaty, led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-punic-war">Third Punic War</a>. He died early in 148 BCE, aged over 90, apparently still leading his armies, without a breach in his alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a> called him the best man of all the kings of his time and credited him with showing that Numidia could produce cultivated fruits; it later became known as the breadbasket of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. Delos honoured him in 179 BCE with a golden crown and a statue after a gift of grain. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/micipsa">Micipsa</a> succeeded him, and his descendants included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juba-i">Juba I</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juba-ii">Juba II</a>. Many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berbers</a> regard him as an icon and forefather.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Masinissa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Masinissa</b>, son of the Massylian king Gaia, was raised partly at Carthage and fought for the Carthaginians in Spain.</blockquote><a href="/empire/numidia">Numidia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>It helps to explain why Numidian and Iberian allies, most prominently the Numidian prince <b>Masinissa</b>, defected to Rome once Carthaginian power began to fail during the Second Punic War.</blockquote><a href="/empire/punic-empire">Punic Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Mauri, Berber peoples of northern Morocco, were united under kings by the late 3rd century BCE; the first well-known king, Baga, provided an escort to <b>Masinissa</b> in 206 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-mauretania">Kingdom of Mauretania</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Masinissa</b> of Numidia seized the Emporia in the 160s BCE, and Leptis later allied with Rome against Jugurtha.</blockquote><a href="/state/leptis-magna">Leptis Magna</a></li><li><blockquote>His son Vermina ruled a reduced western kingdom for a time, but after Zama in 202 BCE most Masaesylian land fell to <b>Masinissa's</b> kingdom of Numidia.</blockquote><a href="/state/masaesyli">Masaesyli</a></li><li><blockquote>King Gaia sent his son <b>Masinissa</b> to fight for Carthage in Spain.</blockquote><a href="/state/massylii">Massylii</a></li></ul></section>
